## What it does

[alchemy-turbo-cache v0.1.0](https://github.com/joelhooks/alchemy-turbo-cache/tree/b38115bee650aceb6e7dd1eb503209a4d32a5381) owns a separate Worker, a dedicated R2 bucket, and generated team credentials.
`packages/turbo-cache` supplies rat-stack's configuration and adoption test.
The Stack includes the [cartridge](/lore/cartridges) with `yield* turboCache`.
The local README describes configuration and removal.

The dependency pins Git commit `b38115bee650aceb6e7dd1eb503209a4d32a5381`.
pnpm prepares its compiled exports during installation through the package-specific `allowBuilds` entry.
No protocol or credential-handoff implementation remains in rat-stack.

The declaration preserves `TurboCache`, `TurboCacheFoundation/TurboCacheBucket`, and `TurboCacheFoundation/TurboCacheToken-rat-stack`.
It adds `TurboCacheFoundation/TurboCacheReaderToken-rat-stack` for the opt-in reader.
The team remains `rat-stack`, with seven-day expiry and prod-only resource retention.
Every entry in `readers` must also appear in `teams`, or cartridge construction fails.

Turbo sends binary task archives through its own HTTP protocol.
The Worker streams those bodies to R2 and streams stored bodies back.
It preserves artifact duration, optional signature tags, and byte lengths.

This is an external protocol [adapter](/lore/hexagonal-architecture).
The [capability projection](/lore/one-capability-every-surface) requires struct inputs, excludes HEAD, and does not describe raw upload streams with per-artifact response headers.
The adapter adds no application capability or alternate agent route.

R2 stores disposable copies of CI outputs under `team/hash` keys.
Turbo's task hash determines reuse.
CI remains responsible for the task result.

## The standard

Every supported route requires a bearer, including status and event acknowledgement.
The Worker compares fixed-length SHA-256 digests with Workers' native constant-time comparison.
It checks every configured credential before selecting a match.

Each token authorizes one team prefix.
A missing or wrong bearer gets 401.
A valid bearer with an unknown or different team gets 403.

Readers may GET and HEAD artifacts, GET status, and POST bulk metadata queries.
All other reader calls return 403, including PUT and event acknowledgement.
Writers retain all supported routes; unsupported routes return 404 for writers.
There are no internal, debug, populate, count, or expiry-override routes.

A writer credential holder can replace artifacts within its team.
Team separation does not protect writers from another writer in the same team.
Only trusted CI should receive writer credentials.

Use readers for deploy builds and worker gates that reuse artifacts without writing them.
Readers can see all cached artifacts within their team.
A reader with the signing key can verify signed artifacts but cannot upload them.
This declaration enables readers; credential distribution remains a separate approved step.

Turbo owns optional signature verification; the Worker preserves the signature tag without verifying archive contents.
rat-stack CI turns signing on with `remoteCache.signature` in `turbo.json` and the `TURBO_REMOTE_CACHE_SIGNATURE_KEY` secret.
Turbo signs every upload and rejects an artifact with a missing or wrong signature, so a leaked cache token or bucket access cannot plant one.

Main and pull requests both use `local:rw,remote:rw`, so a merge whose tree a pull request already built replays from the cache.
A writer able to poison the cache already has push access, and fork pull requests get no token.
The nightly and manual main canary also disable all cache reads.

A red canary beside green cached checks calls for inspecting task inputs and environment declarations.
Other test or runner failures still need diagnosis.

Retention starts at upload.
Reads do not extend an artifact's lifetime.
The default is seven days, configured through an R2 lifecycle rule.
Deletion is asynchronous.

There is no listing cron and no application data in this bucket.

GET responses disable edge caching.
If edge caching is added, deletes will not instantly invalidate those cached responses.
The Worker, bucket, and generated tokens retain only in production.

`TURBO_API` is the origin without `/v8`.
`TURBO_TEAM` is the team slug.
Remote caching stays off unless both token and team are set.

Turbo warns and executes tasks when the cache is unreachable.
A missing token also leaves tasks running without remote reuse.

## How to check

Run `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/turbo-cache test`.
The adoption test starts the installed cartridge in local workerd and checks its exact four resource FQNs.
It hands each generated credential to a subprocess that authenticates against the Worker.
The reader subprocess also checks that PUT returns 403.

The external package owns generated checks for byte-identical reads, team isolation, HEAD agreement, and auth before storage.
Its real Turbo seam uses two empty local cache directories and requires a REMOTE hit on its second run.

The credential handoff reads completed [Alchemy](/skills/learn-alchemy) state in process.
The operator's secret tool must inject `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` and `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` into that process.
The handoff does not load Cloudflare credentials from Alchemy profiles.

Its `CI=true` guard refuses state-store bootstrap and upgrade.
In Alchemy beta.85, that same guard makes environment variables the only credential source.
A failed handoff keeps the source tag and message without serializing credential fields.
Stack deployment authentication is separate from this read-only handoff.

Run `pnpm turbo-cache:token --team rat-stack --stage prod -- <trusted-command>` after the approved deployment.
Add `--role reader` before the receiver separator to select the reader; the default role is `writer`.
Reader handoff requires a completed reader resource and never falls back to the writer.
Set `TURBO_CACHE=remote:r` for reader consumers.

The root script supplies the Stack, state-store, resource prefix and team list to the package's executable.
Only the trusted command's stdin receives the token.

Its stdout and stderr are suppressed.
The parent prints only the API origin and team slug after success.
The desk owns the authorized GitHub secret write.

CI uploads `.turbo/runs/*.json` to separate LOCAL and REMOTE hits.
Check those summaries alongside task counts and the uncached main canary.
Run the full gate with `pnpm turbo run check test build` before shipping.
Alchemy's startup report still bundles the thin cache entry and logs its size.

Local workerd tests do not prove deployed account access, lifecycle deletion, or GitHub credential delivery.
Those remain live checks for the deployment owner.
No deployment is claimed by this page.
